The bank report now also proves an endpoint-specific separator bound.
For a chosen family of a long train rails of at least L edges, a
separator of k source vertices meets at most 5k rails. Pinned mixed
rigidity and positive unloaded equilibrium bound the vertex population
behind the cut by 16*k^2/pi^2+8*k/pi. Hence

    (a-5*k)_+*(L+1) <= 80*k^2/pi^2+40*k/pi.

For the quadratic-core long bank, this forces a linear minimum cut and
therefore a linear family of vertex-disjoint actual contact returns
from specified bank terminals to distinct actual loaded endpoints.
Unlike the conditional near-port/cap packages, this primitive needs
no cap extraction. It still supplies no outside-grid population gain.

The checker runs 27 minimum-cut tests and 113 explicit disjoint contact
returns. Its pinned-count antecedent passes three and fails 24 nonglobal
tests; no positive-stress or loaded-root certification is inferred.
The disjoint paths use the full contact graph and may leave the small
complementary-rail carrier. Combining disjointness with the edge budget,
or proving independently paid lengths or a full-source motion, remains
the next obligation. Level I and the subquadratic-core container branch
remain open.

